  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TZ YEI - OKOTORA (ANAWEZA) PROJECT</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The grant applied to YEI intends to strengthen the ability of young women to advocate and transform Gender Based Violence related Norms. Moreover, the OKOTORA project apply targeted approaches in order to build local capacity and systems to increase gender-based violence (GBV) reporting, provide support to survivors of GBV incidents incidences, and contribute to the ongoing efforts on the ongoing development and implementation of gender-based violence (GBV) policies, particularly the task-shifting policyon GBV services.

Furthermore, the grant applied to YEI in the implementation of the OKOTORA project, it intend to engage the community leaders and educate them on gender-based violence issues, such as the different types of GBV and how to respond to cases involving GBV. A particular focus is on sexual gender-based violence, as this is the type that has the greatest impact on the socioeconomic growth of Tanzania&apos;s women and children. The project intends to use that approach  so that the community becomes a watchdog for the instances of GBV that take place in their community.

In addition, a banner that reads &quot;stop gender-based violence against women in our community&quot; and &quot;Kiswahili Acha Unyanyasaji was kijinsia Kwa wanawake na Watoto&quot; will be flown infront of the offices of the community leaders. Aside from that, instructors will receive training and be equipped to provide this education as part of their course offerings, so that students can be well informed about GBV and be able to report it. In addition, there will be SMART Advocacy training for young women, as well as a variety of social media engagement campaigns using radio talks and utilizing social media platforms to raise awareness and influence changes.
Among the activities that will be implemented include;
1. Inception meeting and Conduct Bilateral meetingwith local government leaders to review gender-based violence policy and activation of gender desk police at ward level.
2. Capacity building and SMART Advocacy training to young womenon GBV, and entrepreneurship skills.
3. Conduct Online and Physical dialogue onGBV awareness to Local leaders and Young women with the entrepreneurship skills.</narrative>
  </description>
